<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  web.xml - Servlet Deployment Descriptor
  This is the entry point for the web application. Tomcat reads this file first
  when the application starts up to configure servlets and filters.
-->
<web-app xmlns="https://jakarta.ee/xml/ns/jakartaee"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="https://jakarta.ee/xml/ns/jakartaee https://jakarta.ee/xml/ns/jakartaee/web-app_6_0.xsd"
         version="6.0">

    <!-- Display name shown in Tomcat manager and other admin tools -->
    <display-name>POS System Spring Web MVC</display-name>

    <!--
      DispatcherServlet Configuration
      This is Spring's front controller that handles all HTTP requests and routes them
      to the appropriate @Controller methods based on @RequestMapping annotations.
    -->
    <servlet>
        <servlet-name>dispatcherServlet</servlet-name>
        <!-- Spring's DispatcherServlet class -->
        <servlet-class>org.springframework.web.servlet.DispatcherServlet</servlet-class>

        <!--
          Use Java-based configuration instead of XML
          AnnotationConfigWebApplicationContext enables @Configuration classes
        -->
        <init-param>
            <param-name>contextClass</param-name>
            <param-value>org.springframework.web.context.support.AnnotationConfigWebApplicationContext</param-value>
        </init-param>

        <!--
          Point to our main Spring configuration class
          This class contains all bean definitions (@Bean methods) and component scanning
        -->
        <init-param>
            <param-name>contextConfigLocation</param-name>
            <param-value>com.pos.config.WebMvcConfig</param-value>
        </init-param>

        <!-- Load this servlet on startup (1 = highest priority) -->
        <load-on-startup>1</load-on-startup>
    </servlet>

    <!--
      Servlet Mapping
      Maps all URL patterns (/) to the DispatcherServlet
      This means ALL requests go through Spring's MVC framework
    -->
    <servlet-mapping>
        <servlet-name>dispatcherServlet</servlet-name>
        <url-pattern>/</url-pattern>
    </servlet-mapping>

</web-app>


<!--

you need web.xml for traditional Spring MVC (unless you switch to Java-based configuration).

Why web.xml Is Needed
Current Setup (Traditional Spring MVC):

web.xml registers the DispatcherServlet with Tomcat
Points to WebMvcConfig.java for Spring configuration
This is the entry point for the web application
Without web.xml:

Tomcat wouldn't know how to load Spring
DispatcherServlet wouldn't be registered
Application wouldn't start
Alternative: Java-Based Configuration
If you want to remove web.xml, you can use DispatcherServletInitializer.java instead:

Enable it by:

Delete web.xml
Modify DispatcherServletInitializer.java:


java
public class DispatcherServletInitializer extends AbstractAnnotationConfigDispatcherServletInitializer {
    @Override
    protected Class<?>[] getRootConfigClasses() {
        return new Class[]{};
    }

    @Override
    protected Class<?>[] getServletConfigClasses() {
        return new Class[]{WebMvcConfig.class};
    }

    @Override
    protected String[] getServletMappings() {
        return new String[]{"/"};
    }
}
Summary
Approach	Files Needed
Current (XML)	web.xml + WebMvcConfig.java
Alternative (Java)	DispatcherServletInitializer.java + WebMvcConfig.java
Spring Boot	None (auto-configured)
Recommendation: Keep web.xml - it's simpler for traditional servlet containers and your current setup is working fine

-->